# Publication commands

The editor adapter owns prompts and a fresh source snapshot, not generation.
Only trusted, local, desktop file workspaces on POSIX are supported, including
LaTeX-only exports: the helper and diagram process lifecycle use POSIX signals.
A global guard rejects overlapping publication commands. A supplied repository
queue serializes source capture and helper cleanup with ordinary generation.
No save-all, cached repository state, or generated source/HTML is used here.


## Registration and configuration

`registerPublicationCommands(context, serialize, bridge)` registers
`literate.exportLatex` and `literate.exportPdf` and attaches their disposables to
the extension context. The optional `PublicationQueue` callback accepts one async
operation and returns its result; without a callback, operations execute directly.
The default bridge runs `out/publication-helper.cjs` as an external Node process.

The restricted, machine-scope string settings have empty defaults:
`literate.publication.nodePath` names a compatible external Node executable,
never Electron; `literate.publication.browserPath` is an optional
absolute sandbox-capable Chromium executable; and
`literate.publication.fontManifest` is optional root-relative JSON.
Executable settings are read only from user/global values, never workspace or
folder overrides. Dependencies such as Pandoc and latexmk/LuaLaTeX remain the
helper's preflight responsibility.
Fonts and licenses are optional disk inputs; editor overlays apply only to sources.

```typescript: <<publication extension imports>>=
import * as vscode from 'vscode';
import * as fs from 'node:fs/promises';
import * as path from 'node:path';
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process';
import { discoveryExcludedDirectories, safeRelativePath } from './engine';
import type { SourceDocument } from './engine';
import { parsePublicationWorkspace, publicationForPath, publicationWorkspaceManifest,
    selectPublication } from './workspace-projects';
import type { PublicationProject, PublicationWorkspace } from './workspace-projects';

type Kind = 'latex' | 'pdf';
interface Choice { paper: string; margins: string; destination: string; }
export interface PublicationRequest {
    args: string[];
    documents: SourceDocument[];
    publicationDocuments?: Record<string, SourceDocument[]>;
}
export interface HelperResult { stdout: string; stderr: string; }
export type PublicationQueue = <T>(operation: () => Promise<T>) => Promise<T>;
export type PublicationBridge = (node: string, helper: string, root: string,
    request: PublicationRequest, token: vscode.CancellationToken,
    report: (message: string) => void) => Promise<HelperResult>;
const papers: Record<string, [number, number]> = {
    a4: [210, 297], a5: [148, 210], letter: [215.9, 279.4], legal: [215.9, 355.6]
};
const excluded = new Set(discoveryExcludedDirectories);
const inputLimit = 32 * 1024 * 1024;
const outputLimit = 8 * 1024 * 1024;
let publishing = false;
const message = (error: unknown): string => error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);
function cancelled(token: vscode.CancellationToken): void {
    if (token.isCancellationRequested) { throw new Error('Publication cancelled'); }
}
```

## Workspace and geometry policy

The editor asks for top, outer, bottom, and inner margins. Inner is the binding edge:
it is left on odd right-hand pages and right on even left-hand pages. Outer exchanges
those physical edges. The extension sends explicit inner/outer CLI options, selecting
a mirrored two-sided book rather than treating left and right as fixed page edges.

Recheck trust and workspace membership at asynchronous boundaries. A save dialog's
initial directory is guidance, not containment enforcement. Each path is checked
lexically and against disk ancestors, including dangling symlinks. Normal OS
filesystem races cannot be eliminated by this adapter; the shared CLI additionally
preflights and protects publication inputs at transaction time.

```typescript: <<publication extension policy>>=
function allowed(folder?: vscode.WorkspaceFolder): void {
    if (!vscode.workspace.isTrusted) { throw new Error('Publication requires Workspace Trust.'); }
    if (vscode.env.remoteName || vscode.env.uiKind === vscode.UIKind.Web) {
        throw new Error('Publication requires a local desktop workspace, not remote or web.');
    }
    if (process.platform === 'win32') { throw new Error('Publication currently requires POSIX.'); }
    if (folder && (folder.uri.scheme !== 'file' || folder.uri.authority ||
        !vscode.workspace.workspaceFolders?.some(item => item.uri.toString() === folder.uri.toString()))) {
        throw new Error('Publication requires a current local file workspace folder.');
    }
}
export function validateMargins(paper: string, value: string): string | undefined {
    const parts = value.split(',').map(part => part.trim());
    const values = parts.map(Number);
    if (parts.length !== 4 || parts.some(part => !/^\d+(?:\.\d+)?$/.test(part)) ||
        values.some(value => !Number.isFinite(value) || value < 5 || value > 50)) {
        return 'Enter four numbers in mm: top,outer,bottom,inner (each 5–50).';
    }
    const size = papers[paper];
    if (!size || size[0] - values[1] - values[3] < 80 || size[1] - values[0] - values[2] < 100) {
        return 'Margins must leave at least an 80 × 100 mm body.';
    }
    return undefined;
}
```

Canonical paths are also used for state keys. Symlink roots are rejected rather
than allowing multiple aliases for the same project. Missing leaf paths are
permitted for new buffers and output destinations, but existing ancestors must
remain real directories.

```typescript: <<publication extension paths>>=
function relativePath(root: string, target: string): string {
    const relative = path.relative(root, target).split(path.sep).join('/');
    const safe = safeRelativePath(relative);
    if (!safe || safe !== relative) { throw new Error(`Path must be inside the selected root: ${target}`); }
    return safe;
}
async function checkPath(root: string, relative: string): Promise<void> {
    let current = root;
    const parts = relative.split('/');
    for (let index = 0; index < parts.length; index++) {
        current = path.join(current, parts[index]);
        let stat;
        try { stat = await fs.lstat(current); }
        catch (error) { if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === 'ENOENT') { return; } throw error; }
        if (stat.isSymbolicLink() || (index < parts.length - 1 ? !stat.isDirectory() : !stat.isFile())) {
            throw new Error(`Unsafe publication path (symlink or non-file): ${current}`);
        }
        if (await fs.realpath(current) !== current) { throw new Error(`Noncanonical path: ${current}`); }
    }
}
async function canonicalRoot(folder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder): Promise<string> {
    allowed(folder);
    const root = path.resolve(folder.uri.fsPath);
    if (await fs.realpath(root) !== root || !(await fs.lstat(root)).isDirectory()) {
        throw new Error('Publication root must be a real, canonical directory, not a symlink.');
    }
    return root;
}
async function canonicalProjectRoot(manifestRoot: string, relativeRoot: string): Promise<string> {
    let root = manifestRoot;
    for (const component of relativeRoot.split('/')) {
        root = path.join(root, component);
        const stat = await fs.lstat(root);
        if (stat.isSymbolicLink() || !stat.isDirectory() || await fs.realpath(root) !== root) {
            throw new Error(`Publication root must be a real, canonical directory: ${relativeRoot}`);
        }
    }
    return root;
}
```

A workspace manifest may live below the VS Code folder so series metadata can be
a sibling of its books. Discovery is bounded, excludes generated and dependency
directories, and never follows symbolic links. More than one manifest is
ambiguous and must be resolved by opening a narrower workspace folder.

```typescript: <<publication extension paths>>=+
async function findPublicationManifest(root: string): Promise<string | undefined> {
    const excluded = `**/{${discoveryExcludedDirectories.join(',')}}/**`;
    const matches = await vscode.workspace.findFiles(
        new vscode.RelativePattern(root, `**/${publicationWorkspaceManifest}`), excluded, 2);
    const found: string[] = [];
    for (const match of matches) {
        if (match.scheme !== 'file' || match.authority) { continue; }
        const relative = relativePath(root, match.fsPath);
        await checkPath(root, relative);
        found.push(match.fsPath);
    }
    if (found.length > 1) { throw new Error(`Multiple ${publicationWorkspaceManifest} files found; open a narrower workspace folder`); }
    return found[0];
}
```

## Fresh source snapshot

All eligible open text is cloned synchronously before the first disk await.
Empty buffers and new file-backed documents override disk just like nonempty
buffers; untitled and foreign documents do not. Discovery is sorted and shares
the engine's directory exclusions. No disk read of an overlaid source is needed.

```typescript: <<publication extension snapshot>>=
export async function capturePublicationSnapshot(root: string, token: vscode.CancellationToken): Promise<SourceDocument[]> {
    cancelled(token);
    const overlays = new Map<string, string>();
    for (const document of vscode.workspace.textDocuments) {
        if (document.uri.scheme !== 'file' || document.uri.authority) { continue; }
        const relative = path.relative(root, document.uri.fsPath).split(path.sep).join('/');
        if (!relative.endsWith('.literate') || relative.startsWith('../') || path.isAbsolute(relative)) { continue; }
        if (relative.split('/').slice(0, -1).some(part => excluded.has(part))) { continue; }
        const safe = relativePath(root, document.uri.fsPath);
        overlays.set(safe, document.getText());
    }
    const documents = new Map<string, string>();
    let bytes = 0;
    const add = (name: string, text: string): void => {
        bytes += Buffer.byteLength(JSON.stringify({ path: name, text })) + 1;
        if (bytes > inputLimit) { throw new Error('Publication snapshot exceeds 32 MiB.'); }
        documents.set(name, text);
    };
    for (const [name, text] of overlays) {
        cancelled(token);
        await checkPath(root, name);
        add(name, text);
    }
```

The final serialized envelope is checked again, including arguments and JSON
escaping. Source files are size-checked before reading; helper input is bounded
independently. Binary assets, manifests, fonts and licenses stay on disk.

```typescript: <<publication extension snapshot>>=+
    const visit = async (directory: string): Promise<void> => {
        cancelled(token);
        const absolute = path.join(root, directory);
        if (await fs.realpath(absolute) !== absolute) { throw new Error(`Noncanonical source directory: ${absolute}`); }
        const entries = await fs.readdir(absolute, { withFileTypes: true });
        entries.sort((a, b) => a.name < b.name ? -1 : a.name > b.name ? 1 : 0);
        for (const entry of entries) {
            cancelled(token);
            if (entry.isSymbolicLink()) { continue; }
            const name = directory ? `${directory}/${entry.name}` : entry.name;
            if (entry.isDirectory() && !excluded.has(entry.name)) { await visit(name); }
            else if (entry.isFile() && name.endsWith('.literate') && !overlays.has(name)) {
                relativePath(root, path.join(root, name));
                await checkPath(root, name);
                if ((await fs.stat(path.join(root, name))).size > inputLimit) {
                    throw new Error(`Publication source exceeds 32 MiB: ${name}`);
                }
                add(name, await fs.readFile(path.join(root, name), 'utf8'));
            }
        }
    };
    await visit('');
    cancelled(token);
    return [...documents].sort(([a], [b]) => a < b ? -1 : a > b ? 1 : 0)
        .map(([name, text]) => ({ path: name, text }));
}
```

## External process lifecycle

Never use a shell or the extension host executable. The helper enforces external
Node >=22.12 and rejects Electron. The bridge bounds combined stdout/stderr and
stdin. Its ten-minute execution deadline allows diagram (120s), TeX (180s) and
preflight budgets, then requests graceful cancellation. Child close acknowledges
termination; exit alone is insufficient because stdio may still be draining.

```typescript: <<publication extension bridge>>=
export const runPublicationHelper: PublicationBridge = (node, helper, root, request, token, report) => {
    cancelled(token);
    const input = JSON.stringify(request);
    if (Buffer.byteLength(input) > inputLimit) { throw new Error('Publication request exceeds 32 MiB.'); }
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        const child = spawn(node, [helper], { cwd: root, shell: false, stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'pipe'] });
        const stdout: Buffer[] = [], stderr: Buffer[] = [];
        let bytes = 0, failure: string | undefined, stopped = false;
        let grace: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
        const stop = (reason?: string): void => {
            failure ??= reason;
            if (stopped) { return; }
            stopped = true;
            if (!child.kill('SIGTERM')) { report('Unable to deliver SIGTERM; waiting for helper exit.'); }
            grace = setTimeout(() => report('Helper has not acknowledged cancellation. Waiting for transaction cleanup; publication remains locked.'), 30000);
        };
        const timer = setTimeout(() => stop('Publication exceeded the 10 minute execution deadline.'), 600000);
        const subscription = token.onCancellationRequested(() => stop());
        const collect = (chunks: Buffer[], chunk: Buffer): void => {
            bytes += chunk.length;
            if (bytes > outputLimit) { stop('Publication helper output exceeds 8 MiB.'); }
            else { chunks.push(chunk); }
        };
        child.stdout.on('data', (chunk: Buffer) => collect(stdout, chunk));
        child.stderr.on('data', (chunk: Buffer) => collect(stderr, chunk));
```

There is deliberately no SIGKILL fallback: terminating a helper mid-rollback can
abandon a transaction. If graceful cleanup stalls, report it and retain the lock
until close rather than claiming cancellation completed or starting another writer.
This is a bounded execution budget, not an unsafe hard cleanup deadline.

```typescript: <<publication extension bridge>>=+
        child.on('error', error => { failure ??= `Cannot run publication Node: ${message(error)}`; });
        child.stdin.on('error', error => stop(`Cannot send publication snapshot: ${message(error)}`));
        child.once('close', (code, signal) => {
            clearTimeout(timer);
            if (grace) { clearTimeout(grace); }
            subscription.dispose();
            const result = { stdout: Buffer.concat(stdout).toString('utf8'), stderr: Buffer.concat(stderr).toString('utf8') };
            if (failure || code !== 0 || signal) {
                reject(new Error([failure ?? `Publication helper exited ${code ?? signal}.`, result.stdout, result.stderr]
                    .filter(Boolean).join('\n')));
            } else {
                try {
                    if (request.publicationDocuments) { publicationBatchOutcome(result, request.args[0]); }
                    else { publicationOutcome(result, request.args[0], request.args[request.args.indexOf('--output') + 1]); }
                    resolve(result);
                } catch (error) { reject(error); }
            }
        });
        if (token.isCancellationRequested) { stop(); }
        if (!stopped) { child.stdin.end(input); }
        else { child.stdin.end(); }
    });
};
```

Cancellation requests SIGTERM but does not overwrite a committed outcome. After
close, exit zero plus a valid written/current response is authoritative, even if
cancellation arrived after commit. Transport errors, deadlines and output overflow
remain failures regardless of exit code or response. Invalid protocol never becomes
success merely because cancellation was requested.

```typescript: <<publication extension bridge>>=+
interface PublicationOutcome { warnings: string[]; elapsedMs?: number; }
function publicationOutcome(result: HelperResult, kind: string, destination: string): PublicationOutcome {
    let response;
    try { response = JSON.parse(result.stdout); }
    catch { throw new Error(`Invalid JSON from publication helper:\n${result.stdout}`); }
    if (response?.schemaVersion !== 1 || response.command !== kind || response.ok !== true ||
        !Array.isArray(response.outputs) || !response.outputs.some((item: { path?: string; status?: string }) =>
            item?.path === destination && ['written', 'current'].includes(item.status ?? ''))) {
        throw new Error(`Publication did not report successful output:\n${result.stdout}`);
    }
    const warnings: unknown = response.publication?.warnings;
    const elapsed = response.elapsedMs;
    return { warnings: Array.isArray(warnings) ? warnings.map(String) : [],
        ...(Number.isInteger(elapsed) && elapsed >= 0 ? { elapsedMs: elapsed } : {}) };
}
function publicationBatchOutcome(result: HelperResult, kind: string): void {
    let response;
    try { response = JSON.parse(result.stdout); }
    catch { throw new Error(`Invalid JSON from publication helper:\n${result.stdout}`); }
    if (response?.schemaVersion !== 1 || response.command !== kind || response.ok !== true ||
        !Array.isArray(response.outputs) || !response.outputs.length ||
        response.outputs.some((item: { path?: string; status?: string }) =>
            typeof item?.path !== 'string' || !['written', 'current'].includes(item.status ?? ''))) {
        throw new Error(`Publication did not report successful batch outputs:\n${result.stdout}`);
    }
}
function seriesPublicationOutcome(result: HelperResult, destinations: string[]): PublicationOutcome {
    let response;
    try { response = JSON.parse(result.stdout); }
    catch { throw new Error(`Invalid JSON from publication helper:\n${result.stdout}`); }
    const outputs = Array.isArray(response?.outputs) ? response.outputs : [];
    if (response?.schemaVersion !== 1 || response.command !== 'pdf' || response.ok !== true ||
        destinations.some(destination => !outputs.some((item: { path?: string; status?: string }) =>
            item?.path === destination && ['written', 'current'].includes(item.status ?? '')))) {
        throw new Error(`Publication did not report successful series outputs:\n${result.stdout}`);
    }
    const warnings: unknown = response.publication?.warnings;
    const elapsed = response.elapsedMs;
    return { warnings: Array.isArray(warnings) ? warnings.map(String) : [],
        ...(Number.isInteger(elapsed) && elapsed >= 0 ? { elapsedMs: elapsed } : {}) };
}
function elapsedPublicationTime(elapsedMs: number): string {
    if (elapsedMs < 1000) { return `${elapsedMs} ms`; }
    const seconds = Math.floor(elapsedMs / 1000);
    return `${String(Math.floor(seconds / 60)).padStart(2, '0')}:${String(seconds % 60).padStart(2, '0')}`;
}
```

## Prompts and explicit dependencies

Remember only successful paper, margins and relative destination, separately for
each canonical root and kind. Every prompt can cancel without spawning or writing.
The active editor's workspace wins; otherwise offer all folders explicitly.

When Export PDF discovers a workspace manifest, that manifest denotes one ordered
series operation rather than a publication choice. Every project must declare
`pdf` before snapshots or helper work begin. The adapter then invokes the helper
once with `pdf <manifest-root> --json`. The request carries project-relative
snapshots keyed by publication ID; the helper reads paper, margins, and every
manifest-relative output from the manifest. Snapshots remain isolated, while one
helper lifecycle compiles the complete series and commits its PDFs together.
Manifest-relative PDF paths may resolve outside a publication root. Export LaTeX
and workspaces without a manifest retain the interactive single-publication
protocol.

```typescript: <<publication extension prompts>>=
async function chooseFolder(): Promise<vscode.WorkspaceFolder | undefined> {
    allowed();
    const active = vscode.window.activeTextEditor?.document.uri;
    const folder = active && vscode.workspace.getWorkspaceFolder(active);
    if (folder) { allowed(folder); return folder; }
    const folders = vscode.workspace.workspaceFolders ?? [];
    if (!folders.length) { throw new Error('Open a local project folder before exporting.'); }
    const choice = await vscode.window.showQuickPick(folders.map(folder => ({
        label: folder.name, description: folder.uri.fsPath, folder
    })), { placeHolder: 'Select publication workspace folder' });
    if (choice) { allowed(choice.folder); }
    return choice?.folder;
}
interface SelectedPublication {
    folder: vscode.WorkspaceFolder;
    workspaceRoot: string;
    root: string;
    project?: PublicationProject;
    series?: PublicationWorkspace;
}
async function choosePublication(kind: Kind): Promise<SelectedPublication | undefined> {
    const folder = await chooseFolder();
    if (!folder) { return undefined; }
    const folderRoot = await canonicalRoot(folder);
    const manifestPath = await findPublicationManifest(folderRoot);
    if (!manifestPath) { return { folder, workspaceRoot: folderRoot, root: folderRoot }; }
    const workspaceRoot = path.dirname(manifestPath);
    await checkPath(workspaceRoot, publicationWorkspaceManifest);
    const workspace = parsePublicationWorkspace(await fs.readFile(manifestPath, 'utf8'));
    if (kind === 'pdf') {
        const missing = workspace.publications.filter(project => !project.pdf).map(project => project.id);
        if (missing.length) { throw new Error(`Every publication must declare pdf for series export; missing: ${missing.join(', ')}`); }
        return { folder, workspaceRoot, root: workspaceRoot, series: workspace };
    }
    const active = vscode.window.activeTextEditor?.document.uri;
    const relative = active?.scheme === 'file' && !active.authority ?
        path.relative(workspaceRoot, active.fsPath).split(path.sep).join('/') : undefined;
    let project = relative && !path.isAbsolute(relative) ? publicationForPath(workspace, relative) : undefined;
    if (!project && workspace.publications.length === 1) { project = selectPublication(workspace); }
    if (!project) {
        const choice = await vscode.window.showQuickPick(workspace.publications.map(item => ({
            label: item.title, description: item.id, detail: item.root, project: item
        })), { placeHolder: 'Select publication' });
        project = choice?.project;
    }
    if (!project) { return undefined; }
    return { folder, workspaceRoot, root: await canonicalProjectRoot(workspaceRoot, project.root), project };
}
function executable(name: string, required: boolean): string | undefined {
    const value = vscode.workspace.getConfiguration('literate.publication').inspect<string>(name)?.globalValue;
    if (value === undefined || value === '') {
        if (required) { throw new Error(`Set literate.publication.${name} in User settings to an absolute external Node >=22.12 executable (not Electron).`); }
        return undefined;
    }
    if (typeof value !== 'string' || !path.isAbsolute(value) || /[\x00-\x1f]/.test(value)) {
        throw new Error(`literate.publication.${name} must be an explicit absolute executable path in User settings.`);
    }
    return value;
}
```

Paper ordering makes the last successful value the initial selection without
silently bypassing the prompt. Validate margins both in the input UI and after it
returns, and enforce the save URI's scheme, extension and containment ourselves.

```typescript: <<publication extension prompts>>=+
async function chooseOutput(root: string, kind: Kind, previous?: Choice): Promise<Choice | undefined> {
    const names = Object.keys(papers);
    if (previous && names.includes(previous.paper)) { names.splice(names.indexOf(previous.paper), 1); names.unshift(previous.paper); }
    const paper = await vscode.window.showQuickPick(names, { placeHolder: 'Publication page size' });
    if (paper === undefined) { return undefined; }
    const margins = await vscode.window.showInputBox({
        prompt: 'Margins in mm: top, outer, bottom, inner/binding (5–50 each)',
        value: previous?.margins ?? '20,20,20,20', validateInput: value => validateMargins(paper, value) });
    if (margins === undefined) { return undefined; }
    const error = validateMargins(paper, margins);
    if (error) { throw new Error(error); }
    const extension = kind === 'latex' ? 'tex' : 'pdf';
    const remembered = previous?.destination;
    const destination = remembered && safeRelativePath(remembered) === remembered && remembered.endsWith(`.${extension}`)
        ? remembered : `publication.${extension}`;
    const uri = await vscode.window.showSaveDialog({ defaultUri: vscode.Uri.file(path.join(root, destination)),
        filters: { [extension.toUpperCase()]: [extension] }, title: 'Export publication inside the selected project' });
    if (!uri) { return undefined; }
    if (uri.scheme !== 'file' || uri.authority || !uri.fsPath.endsWith(`.${extension}`)) {
        throw new Error(`Choose a local .${extension} file inside the selected project.`);
    }
    const relative = relativePath(root, uri.fsPath);
    await checkPath(root, relative);
    return { paper, margins: margins.split(',').map(value => value.trim()).join(','), destination: relative };
}
```

## Command lifetime

The global guard covers prompts, queue waiting, snapshot, helper cleanup, and
remembering the successful choice. It is released before the optional Open Output
notification, which is not publication work and must not block another export.
Each operation owns its cancellation source. Removing its selected workspace folder
latches authority revocation and cancels the source, even if the folder is re-added.
The optional bridge parameter isolates process behavior in headless tests.

```typescript: <<publication extension commands>>=
export function registerPublicationCommands(context: vscode.ExtensionContext,
    serialize: PublicationQueue = operation => operation(), bridge: PublicationBridge = runPublicationHelper): void {
    const output = vscode.window.createOutputChannel('Literate Publication');
    let disposed = false;
    let active: { source: vscode.CancellationTokenSource; folder?: vscode.WorkspaceFolder; revoked?: string } | undefined;
    const foldersChanged = vscode.workspace.onDidChangeWorkspaceFolders(event => {
        if (active?.folder && event.removed.some(folder => folder.uri.toString() === active!.folder!.uri.toString())) {
            active.revoked = 'Selected workspace folder was removed.';
            active.source.cancel();
        }
    });
    const execute = async (kind: Kind): Promise<void> => {
        if (disposed) { return; }
        if (publishing) {
            await vscode.window.showWarningMessage('A publication is already in progress. Wait for it to finish.');
            return;
        }
        publishing = true;
        const operation: NonNullable<typeof active> = { source: new vscode.CancellationTokenSource() };
        active = operation;
        const token = operation.source.token;
        let completed: string | undefined;
        const authority = (): void => {
            if (disposed) { throw new Error('Publication registration was disposed.'); }
            if (operation.revoked) { throw new Error(operation.revoked); }
            allowed(operation.folder);
        };
```

Prompts do not hold the repository queue. Disposal during prompts still cancels
the operation; no subsequent snapshot or helper may start. Executable settings
are resolved only after a destination has been chosen.

```typescript: <<publication extension commands>>=+
        try {
            const selected = await choosePublication(kind);
            if (!selected) { return; }
